How to Make the Most of These Two Handbooks

Origin
In the 21st century, humanity has entered an era of material abundance yet inner impoverishment. While material and technological advancements have brought great convenience to daily life, they have also given rise to global ecological challenges such as environmental pollution and climate change, as well as psychological struggles including anxiety and depression. Spiritual emptiness, inner unrest, and the resulting social crises have become shared challenges that humanity is facing. Meanwhile, frequent international conflicts continue to unsettle the world, and the rapid advancement of artificial intelligence carries the potential to replace human roles across many professions. These increasingly accelerated external changes have generated a profound sense of uncertainty, leaving many people feeling anxious and confused about the future.
The root of these challenges lies in an excessive focus on external achievements, while neglecting three fundamental questions of human life: What is the unique value of being human? What is the meaning of life? How can modern people find peace in body and mind? If these questions are not honestly faced and addressed, even material abundance cannot bring inner peace. How, then, can these questions be answered? In this regard, traditional Chinese culture offers distinctive strengths. In particular, the inward-oriented wisdom emphasized in Buddhism provides unique value for understanding the nature of the mind, seeing clearly the truth of life, transforming mistaken views, cultivating healthy mindsets, developing an integrated character, and contributing to both personal and collective well-being. If these strengths can be brought into full play, they will offer renewed hope for the world and for humanity today.
To address these challenges, Venerable Master Jiqun, drawing on over forty years of personal cultivation and realization, as well as  more than thirty years of Dharma teaching, has established the Mindful Peace Academy Curriculum. This curriculum is characterized by its accessibility, systematic structure, practical methods, and verifiable results. It is specifically designed to address the key challenges in Buddhist study and practice, offering a path tailored to the needs of modern individuals.
Buddha Dharma is profound and intricate, encompassing vast scriptures. Without systematic guidance, practitioners may struggle to benefit despite their efforts and time, eventually losing motivation to delve deeper. Even with steadfast faith and diligence, limited understanding of Buddha Dharma can lead to a narrow and biased perspective, hindering further progress.
Moreover, some practitioners choose to study independently, either lacking access to virtuous teachers or overestimating their own abilities. Relying on personal intuition, they attempt to delve into the scriptures and explore practice on their own. Yet, faced with the vastness of the Buddhist canon and the diverse methods, they often end up losing their way.  What’s worse, some may become arrogant and overconfident upon acquiring only a fraction of knowledge, which becomes a significant obstacle in their practice. As a result, many practitioners fall into extremes: either missing the essence, or becoming rigid and one-sided, or slipping into blind and unguided practice, or drifting into abstract theorizing.
In addition to deviations in individual practice, the propagation of the Dharma also faces difficulties such as being out of touch with the times, misunderstood, and even marginalized. During the Republican era in China, Master Taixu advocated Humanistic Buddhism, bringing Buddhism into the sphere of everyday life, encouraging engagement with social issues, and embodying a spirit of compassion dedicated to benefiting society.  His work significantly popularized Buddhist teachings. However, if the essence of Humanistic Buddhism is not properly understood, it can be reduced to a mere concern with present–life well-being, while losing sight of liberation as its fundamental aim. This shift tends to reduce Buddhism to a superficial and secularized practice.
In April 2006, at the inaugural World Buddhist Forum, the Master presented "One Foundation, Three Key Elements," offering an effective solution. The foundation is to construct an accessible curriculum for study and practice suited to contemporary practitioners.
![](media/image7.png){width="3.5433048993875764in" height="0.36909667541557306in"}1
On this foundation, three systems are established: Buddhist education, Sangha management, and Dharma propagation. Subsequently, the Master published a series of papers including "Reflections on Chinese Buddhism," "Reflections on the Stages of Buddhist Practice," and "My Approach to Classifying the Teachings," elaborating on the core components, stages, and practical methods that an accessible curriculum must include. The Master holds that without an accessible curriculum that is both true to the Dharma and relevant to the times — one that is practical and actionable — practitioners will struggle, lack inner support from the Dharma, and may even lose faith and aspiration, giving rise to all kinds of problems and crises. Once the foundation is secured and study and practice are on track, many issues will resolve themselves. These reflections have generated significant resonance both within and beyond Buddhist communities.
“Accessibility” has two layers of meaning. First, it means the program is suitable for all practitioners – whether monastic or lay, beginner or experienced. Second, it is compatible with all Buddhist teachings and can enrich any form of practice. The focus on accessibility is crucial due to the scarcity of virtuous teachers. For many Buddhist practitioners, following the guidance of virtuous teachers has become more of a hope than a reality. In such a context, only an accessible, standardized, and systematic approach to Buddhist study and practice can facilitate widespread accessibility. This maximizes the use of available resources and provides systematic and effective guidance for those interested in learning Buddhism. The systematic implementation of such an accessible study and practice system is not just a remedy for current challenges; it also represents a new exploration in the modern dissemination of Buddhism.
A “systematic” approach refers to a comprehensive study and practice system that includes core components, stages of practice, and practical methods. Despite the vastness and profundity of the Dharma, its core components focus on Taking Refuge, Generating Aspiration, Observing Precepts, Establishing Right View, and Practicing Samatha-Vipassana. These elements are crucial for both the path of liberation and the bodhisattva path. Taking refuge is the foundation for becoming a Buddhist. Generating aspiration provides direction in spiritual practice. Observing precepts establishes a foundation for developing concentration and wisdom. Right View serves as the Dharma eye, while Samatha-Vipassana provides the methods of practice; both are indispensable for cultivation. By grasping these core components, you will understand the essential content of the practice. Furthermore, it is vital to comprehend the practical sequence and correlation among these components. Taking Refuge, Generating Aspiration, and Observing Precepts form the common foundation for all Dharma practices. Regardless of the school you follow, you must first establish these fundamentals. Then, select a specific school’s right view and methods of Samatha-Vipassana for deeper practice.
“Practical” and “verifiable” mean that each stage of practice must have its steps and procedures, match specific learning methods, and allow practitioners to assess their progress. This way, practitioners can see the results of their efforts, understand where they are on the path, and know how far they are from the goal.
Based on these principles, the Mindful Peace Academy curriculum is structured into three levels—beginner, intermediate, and advanced. Each level has clear objectives, comprehensive content, a well-ordered progression, and corresponding guidance for practice.
The beginner level focuses on exploring the true meaning of life and establishing a life guided by wisdom. Through the Wisdom in Life course, students are guided to overcome  misunderstandings about the Dharma, learn to view the world and life through its wisdom, and begin to resolve coarse forms of affliction. Through the practice of beginner-level mindfulness meditation and beginner-level altruistic meditation, students begin to cultivate Chan-inspired living and a life of wisdom. In doing so, they draw closer to the Buddha, explore the true meaning of life, and begin the search for the ultimate destination of life.
The intermediate level is grounded in progressive study and practice, cultivating mindfulness and compassion. Through studying treatises such as *The Medium Treatise on the Stages of the Path to Enlightenment*, *Lucid Introduction to the One Hundred Dharmas*, *Discussion of the Cultivation of Antidotes*, *The Way of the Bodhisattva*, and *The Yogacara Bodhisattva Precepts*, students understand and master the essentials and progression of the bodhisattva path. Building on beginner-level mindfulness meditation, students then undertake intermediate-level mindfulness meditation grounded in the Thirty-Seven Limbs of Enlightenment.
![](media/image8.png){width="3.5433048993875764in" height="0.36909667541557306in"}2
Moreover, in accordance with the Mahayana spirit of Buddhism, the curriculum places special emphasis on the teaching of bodhicitta. Through proper study and practice, students undertake intermediate-level altruistic meditation on the foundation of beginner-level altruistic meditation, benefiting themselves and others, awakening themselves and others.
The advanced level establishes the right view of emptiness and undertakes emptiness meditation. Through listening and contemplation of the scriptures, students establish right view and practice samatha and vipassana. Building on intermediate-level mindfulness and intermediate-level altruistic meditation, they undertake advanced-level mindfulness meditation and advanced-level altruistic meditation, giving rise to the wisdom of emptiness and realizing ultimate bodhicitta.
To support the entire curriculum, the Master has designed a systematic methodology of "one path, two dimensions, four roles, five areas of mindful application, and three types of meditation." In particular, the 18-Character Cultivation Guideline, the Eight Steps and Three Types of Meditation (hereafter "Eight Steps, Three Meditations"), mindfulness meditation, and altruistic meditation — by following this methodology, students can quickly benefit from their study and practice. Those who lack the conditions to complete the full curriculum may choose to study in stages according to their circumstances.
Since its launch, the Mindful Peace Academy curriculum has been warmly received by practitioners, and to date, a great many people of insight have undertaken study and practice through it. After more than a decade of testing through widespread practice, this curriculum has proven highly effective and can truly serve as a path of awakening, guiding countless Buddhist practitioners in exploring the truth of life.
The study philosophy of Mindful Peace Academy rests on three guiding principles. First, empower everyone to take initiative in their study and practice — not passively receiving instruction, but actively charting their own path of awakening. Each person is the master of their own life and the master of their own study and practice. Second, enable everyone to contribute fully — study and practice benefit not only oneself but also others in the class, the Academy, and its programs. Every sharing, every act of service, both fulfills oneself and brings light to others. Third, make study and practice easier — by providing clear methods, helpful tools, and a warm and supportive environment. Practice is not an ascetic ordeal; it is a bright path with a map, companions, and places to refuel along the way.
This world is deeply interconnected. To bring peace of body and mind to people at home and abroad, and to foster harmony between humanity and the world, the voice of Eastern wisdom needs to be heard more widely. Mindful Peace Academy is a brand of Eastern wisdom culture established by Mindful Peace International (MPI). With its purely non-profit mindfulness curriculum as the core offering, it provides two models: guidance in study and practice, and service to the community. Through the study-and-practice guidance model, students engage in mindfulness meditation to grow in wisdom. Through the community-service model, they engage in altruistic meditation to nurture compassion. In this way, students are supported in embracing a Chan-inspired way of living and walking the path of an awakened life — awakening oneself and others, benefiting the world.
Let us become transmitters of wisdom — bringing the culture of inner peace back to our families, into our communities, bringing light to those who are lost and a sense of calm to this restless world. May we at Mindful Peace Academy not only learn wisdom but live it. May we not only light the lamp in our own hearts but help many others light theirs as well.
Finally, this handbook is not a book of “rules and regulations,” but a roadmap for growth. Its purpose is not to restrict you, but to help each person walk the path of awakening with greater clarity and ease.
