Student Guidelines
Conduct Guidelines for Respecting the Sangha and Monasteries
Dear participants,
As participants in study and daily life, we may visit different monasteries, encounter monastics from various traditions, or come into contact with diverse approaches to practice. As members of Mindful Peace Academy, we should uphold a grounded confidence in the Three Jewels and to conduct ourselves with clarity and respect in these situations.
1. Respect All Monastics
Monastics are not only teachers of gods and humans but also pillars who sustain the lifeblood of the Dharma and symbols of the enduring presence of the true Dharma. Without the Sangha, the Buddhadharma could not have been transmitted in this world. Participants should respect and revere all monastics with a reverent heart.
2. Observe Monastery Etiquette
(1) Upon entering a monastery, first pay respect to the Buddha in the proper manner. When encountering a monastic, greet them with palms joined.
(2) When making prostrations to a Dharma teacher: if the teacher says "a half-bow will do," then a half-bow is sufficient; if the teacher says "one prostration," then one prostration is sufficient; if the teacher says nothing, three prostrations are appropriate.
(3) Dress modestly and appropriately. Women should not enter the monastery in revealing, tight, or transparent clothing. When conversing, do not tug at the teacher's robes. When taking photographs, do not stand too close to the teacher, so as to avoid causing criticism or misunderstanding. The same applies to men visiting nuns' monasteries.
(4) Do not create noise or disturbance within the monastery. Avoid joking around with monastics, especially in public settings. When exchanging insights or Dharma points, speak in a gentle and soft voice.
(5) When accompanying a monastic on foot, walk behind them—neither too close nor too far. When speaking with a monastic, maintain mindfulness of body and mind, and be careful in word and deed.
3. Guard Your Speech and Refrain from Gossip
Under any circumstances, toward other monasteries, traditions, and monastics, apart from offering respect and praise, do not make careless judgments or spread gossip.
4. Respect the Path Others Follow
When introducing the merits of Mindful Peace Academy, you may objectively share your own insights and transformation. If the other person already has a teacher and tradition they follow, encourage them to deepen their practice. Do not disparage other traditions or actively steer anyone toward changing their path.
5. Respect Monasteries
Every monastery has its own distinct orientation. We should first and foremost show respect and never make careless judgments or rank them as higher or lower. When holding group practice or other activities at another monastery, remain grateful in heart and proper in word and deed. Beyond the scope of your group practice or activity there, do not actively promote Mindful Peace Academy or share the Dharma with that monastery's lay supporters, so as to avoid unnecessary misunderstandings. However, if the abbot, monastics, or lay supporters of that monastery show genuine interest in Mindful Peace Academy and ask of their own accord, you may explain the Academy's framework to them in detail.
![](media/image7.png){width="3.5433048993875764in" height="0.36909667541557306in"}27
6. Respect Your Original Refuge Teacher
Some participants took refuge under other monasteries or teachers before joining Mindful Peace Academy. This does not conflict with following the Master's guidance in study, because we take refuge in the Three Jewels of the ten directions. Therefore, continue to respect your original refuge teacher without any concern. In your study here, treasure the conditions at Mindful Peace Academy, settle into the framework, and practice diligently.
7. Do Not Introduce Other Traditions Within the Study Community
The Buddhadharma is vast as the ocean, yet our energy and wisdom are limited. In light of this, the Master has specially designed the Mindful Peace Academy study system—a practice framework tailored for modern people that maximizes effectiveness. Joining Mindful Peace Academy means you identify with this framework. Only by establishing an attitude of "sincere, earnest, honest, and proactive" study can you safeguard the quality of individual practice and the atmosphere of group practice. Therefore, during your study period, do not restlessly seek elsewhere, and do not introduce other teachers or traditions within the study community. Otherwise, not only will you struggle to benefit yourself, but you will also disrupt others' practice. Please take this to heart.
8. Maintain Good Relationships with Local Monasteries and Elders
Monasteries are religious activity sites protected by law, fully endowed with the Three Jewels and powerfully blessed. If we can serve the monastery through our study framework and Dharma-sharing approach, it is our good fortune. Therefore, make an effort to maintain good relationships with local monasteries and elders, gaining their recognition to facilitate the smooth conduct of Dharma-sharing and study activities.

Each participant represents the effectiveness of Mindful Peace Academy. I hope we can truly value this opportunity—so that, having benefited from the Dharma, we embody the teachings in our conduct, benefit both ourselves and others, and form positive and meaningful connections.
Empowerment Channels
When you encounter difficulties, here is what you can do—
On the path of study and practice, everyone faces confusion, lapses in motivation, or plateaus. This is not failure; it is a necessary stage of growth.
When you feel challenged, you are not alone. The following channels can help you:
Talk to your class facilitator: Your class facilitator is your running partner, available to talk at any time. The facilitator's responsibility is to help you grow.
Talk to fellow practitioners: Class group practice and small-group practice are the best support systems. The confusion you face may be something others have already been through.
Join forums or round-table discussions: The regional association regularly organizes themed forums to address common challenges. You can go with questions and return with answers.
![](media/image8.png){width="3.5433048993875764in" height="0.36909667541557306in"}28
Join a club: Mindful Tea, Mindfulness Ball, reading groups, health and wellness. Find companions through shared interests and stay immersed in the practice in a relaxed way.
Use the "Path to Awakening" platform: Global best-practice examples, practitioner highlights, and project-based courses—learn what you need, whenever you need it.
Use tools such as the Bodhi Navigator and MetaNote: Self-check, record, and navigate—making your study and practice visible and trackable.
Remember: Asking for help is not weakness; it is wisdom. Sharing is not showing off; it is benefiting others.
When we empower each other and grow together, this path to awakening grows ever brighter.
When you encounter challenges, you may try the following
The Five Wonderful Mindfulness Training for Healthy Living
The First Training: Not Killing
Aware of the suffering caused by the destruction of life, I am committed to cultivating compassion and learning ways to protect the lives of people, animals, and plants. I am determined not to kill, not to let others kill, and not to condone any act of killing in the world, in my thinking, or in my way of life. Nor will I rejoice in any act of killing by anyone.
The Second Training: Not Stealing
Aware of the suffering caused by exploitation, oppression, stealing, and social injustice, I am committed to cultivating loving-kindness and learning ways to work for the well-being of people, animals, and plants. I will practice generosity by sharing my time, energy, and material resources with those who are truly in need. I am determined not to steal and not to possess anything that should belong to others. I will respect the property of others, but I will prevent others from profiting from human suffering or the suffering of other species on Earth.
The Third Training: Not Engaging in Sexual Misconduct
Aware of the suffering caused by sexual misconduct, I am committed to cultivating responsibility and learning ways to protect the safety and integrity of individuals, couples, families, and society. I am determined not to engage in sexual relations without love and a long-term commitment. To preserve the happiness of myself and others, I am determined to respect my own commitments and the commitments of others. I will do everything in my power to protect children from sexual abuse and to prevent couples and families from being broken by sexual misconduct.
The Fourth Training: Not Lying
Aware of the suffering caused by unmindful speech and the inability to listen to others, I am committed to cultivating loving speech and deep listening in order to bring joy and happiness to others and relieve them of their suffering. Knowing that words can create happiness or suffering, I am committed to speaking truthfully, with words that inspire self-confidence, joy, and hope. I am determined not to spread uncertain information, not to criticize or condemn things of which I am not sure, and to avoid words that may cause division or discord, or that may cause the family or the community to break. I will make every effort to reconcile and resolve all conflicts, however small.
![](media/image7.png){width="3.5433048993875764in" height="0.36909667541557306in"}29

The Fifth Training: Not Consuming Intoxicants
Aware of the suffering caused by unmindful consumption, I am committed to cultivating good health, both physical and mental, for myself, my family, and my society by practicing mindful eating, drinking, and consuming. I will ingest only items that preserve peace, well-being, and joy in my body, in my consciousness, and in the collective body and consciousness of my family and society. I am determined not to use alcohol, not to consume unhealthy foods, and not to engage with unwholesome mental products such as certain television programs, magazines, books, films, and conversations. I am aware that to damage my body or my consciousness with these "poisons" is to betray my ancestors, my parents, my society, and future generations.
I will practice the Five Mindfulness Training for Healthy Living that apply both to individuals and society to transform violence, fear, anger, and chaos within myself and the world around me. I understand that to change myself and the world, a proper code of precepts is essential.
Dharma Music and Chants
Metta Chanting
May I be free from enmity and danger, may I be free from mental suffering, may I be free from physical suffering, may I be well and happy.
May my parents, my teachers, relatives and friends, and my fellow practitioners be free from enmity and danger, may they be free from mental suffering, may they be free from physical suffering, may they be well and happy.
May all meditators in this temple be free from enmity and danger, may they be free from mental suffering, may they be free from physical suffering, may they be well and happy.
May the monks, novices, laymen and laywomen in this temple be free from enmity and danger, may they be free from mental suffering, may they be free from physical suffering, may they be well and happy.
May our donors of the four requisites be free from enmity and danger, may they be free from mental suffering, may they be free from physical suffering, may they be well and happy.
May our guardian deities — in this temple, in this dwelling, in this vicinity — may all guardian deities be free from enmity and danger, may they be free from mental suffering, may they be free from physical suffering, may they be well and happy.
May all beings, all living beings, all beings with form, all beings with name and appearance, all beings with bodies, all females, all males, all noble ones, all worldlings, all devas,
![](media/image8.png){width="3.5433048993875764in" height="0.36909667541557306in"}30
May all humans, all beings in the realms of suffering be free from enmity and danger, may they be free from mental suffering, may they be free from physical suffering, may they be well and happy. May all beings be released from suffering. May they not lose what they have rightfully obtained. May they inherit the results of their own karma.
In the east, in the west, in the north, in the south, in the southeast, in the northwest, in the northeast, in the southwest, below, above — may all beings, all living beings,
all beings with form, all beings with name and appearance, all beings with bodies, all females, all males, all noble ones, all worldlings, all devas, all humans, all beings in the realms of suffering — may they be free from enmity and danger, may they be free from mental suffering, may they be free from physical suffering, may they be well and happy. May all beings be released from suffering. May they not lose what they have rightfully obtained. May they inherit the results of their own karma.
From the highest heavens to the beings in the realms of suffering, all beings in the three realms, all beings dwelling on land — may they be free from mental suffering and enmity, may they be free from physical suffering and danger.
From the highest heavens to the beings in the realms of suffering, all beings in the three realms, all beings dwelling in water — may they be free from mental suffering and enmity, may they be free from physical suffering and danger.
From the highest heavens to the beings in the realms of suffering, all beings in the three realms, all beings dwelling in air — may they be free from mental suffering and enmity, may they be free from physical suffering and danger.
Song of the Three Jewels
(Lyrics by Master Taixu, music by Master Hongyi)
Long is the night for humans and devas, the universe in darkness — who brings forth the light? The three realms are a burning house, all forms of suffering press in — who brings peace and serenity? Great compassion, great wisdom, great heroic power — Namo Buddhaya! Illuminating all existence, sheltering all beings, virtues beyond all words. Now we know: this alone is the true refuge. For as long as life lasts, we offer body and life — with faith we receive and diligently practice!
The two truths encompass all, the three trainings ever deepening — vast is the Dharmakaya. Perfect in pure virtue, all defilements stilled — boundless is the city of Nirvana. All conditions are empty, only consciousness appears — Namo Dharmaya! No principle left unillumined, no delusion left undispelled — radiant is its great light! Now we know: this alone is the true refuge. For as long as life lasts, we offer body and life — with faith we receive and diligently practice!
Through pure precepts, achieving wondrous harmony — the fragrant legacy of the Vulture Peak. Cultivating practice and realizing the fruits, spreading the Dharma and benefiting the world — the flame of the Buddha's lamp continues to burn. How countless are the sages of the Three Vehicles — Namo Sanghaya! Guiding the assembly, unobstructed in all things, upholding the citadel of the true Dharma! Now we know: this alone is the true refuge. For as long as life lasts, we offer body and life — with faith we receive and diligently practice!
O Buddha, We Have Come Home
Neither coming nor going, thus come, thus gone — through time and space, your Dharma voice ever abides. Neither coming nor going, thus come, thus gone — through time and space, you have never left. Drawing my heart close to your embrace, compassion's power surrounds like boundless space. Following your wisdom's
![](media/image7.png){width="3.5433048993875764in" height="0.36909667541557306in"}31
footprints, the lotus of awakening blooms one by one.
O Buddha! O Buddha! We have finally come home. O Buddha! O Buddha! Your children have come home.
The Tathagata's Messenger
Who is it, crossing through the mists of a thousand years, riding here on a great vow?
Who is it, lighting the eternal lamp, guiding the once-lost path home?
Amidst the clamor of the dusty world, the Dharma voice resounds without end; amidst worldly turmoil, the mind's lamp passes on unbroken.
Beneath the Bodhi tree, sowing seeds of wisdom's words; where the lotus blooms, the world becomes a pure land.
Following
Have I met you before? You smile and say nothing — sunlight pours through the heart and flowers bloom.
It must be you — I have found you at last. A gentle breeze sweeps through heaven and earth without end.
How many times have I been lost in samsara, yet how many times have I suddenly turned back.
But you were always there behind me, never once far away, no matter when.
I cannot help but be like a child, smiling foolishly yet tears welling up.
The fortress of my heart lets down its guard — this deep and lasting joy lingers on.
Have I met you before? You smile and say nothing — sunlight pours through the heart and flowers bloom.
It must be you — I have found you at last. A gentle breeze sweeps through heaven and earth without end.
Ah, struggling bitterly in samsara, racing through life and death unable to break free.
It is your boundless compassion and wisdom that lead me out of these heavy chains.
Ah, warm tears stream down my face — the child who had lost the way has found home today.
On the Bodhi path, following in your footsteps, together we shall behold the flowers of the other shore.
It is you who made aspiration to seek perfect awakening, forgetting yourself to help all beings.
I only wish to follow in your steps, life after life never again to be apart, life after life never again to be apart.
Bodhi Blossoms
If you thirst for a drop of water, I would pour out an entire ocean for you.
If you wish to pick a single red leaf, I would give you the whole maple forest and the clouds above.
![](media/image8.png){width="3.5433048993875764in" height="0.36909667541557306in"}32
If you want a smile, I open my heart's warm embrace. If you need someone to walk beside you, I will journey with you into the future. Bodhi blossoms — this is the world of awakening.
Each group practice awakens my love for all beings.
Bodhi blossoms — life flows like water through samsara. Truly awakening and awakening others is my offering to the Three Jewels.
Boundless compassionate vows, always with us. Bodhi blossoms — this is the world of awakening.
Life flows like water — sometimes calm, sometimes surging. Crossing through samsara, purity and joy are everywhere. In truth, awakening has always been with us. In our world, Bodhi blossoms.
Watching the Clouds Arise
Watching the clouds arise.
Not wanting to miss that cloudless sky, you say: child, learn to let go — like a fallen leaf settling into the grass. Watching the clouds arise.
The stone-paved path bears your serene ease.
Mountains, rivers, the great earth — nothing but a dream.
May I walk out of my own maze.
Always dazed and drifting, lost and confused.
Following you, roasting taro, sipping tea, listening to the pines — then off to the fields to feel the warm breeze.
Tap my head — this too is a dream. The world's affairs drift on and on — better a quiet hillside. Pines shade the sun, clear streams flow forever. Life and death, no longer a concern — what else is there to worry about?
![](media/image7.png){width="3.5433048993875764in" height="0.36909667541557306in"}33
Watching you come and go, utterly free.
Watching the clouds arise.
No longer pulled by reality into the black hole.
Perhaps the road ahead is riddled with holes.
You say: with faith, no winter is too harsh.
Melting ice and snow — light shining in the darkness.
No more dazed and drifting, lost and confused.
Still roasting taro, sipping tea, listening to the pines.
Why still ponder east, west, south, north, or centre?
Call it anything and you miss the mark.
The world's affairs drift on and on — better a quiet hillside.
Pines shade the sun, clear streams flow forever.
Life and death, no longer a concern — what else is there to worry about?
Watching you come and go, utterly free.
Happy Birthday, Dharma Brother
Happy birthday to you, dear Dharma brother.
On this unforgettable day, I sing a song for you. Along this path, from meeting to knowing — do you still remember? Our promise to awaken.
Grateful for you, for your selfless giving. Thankful for you, for holding the umbrella in the rain. Moved by you, for the warmth within our class. With shared vows we walk together, supporting each other on this path.
Auspicious birthday to you, dear Dharma brother.
On this unforgettable day, let us share from the heart. Through the changing seasons, you remain unchanged. Do you still remember? Our Bodhi promise.
Grateful for you, for your selfless giving. Thankful for you, for holding the umbrella in the rain. Moved by you, for the warmth within our class. Never leaving, never forsaking — we watch the clouds arise together.
(A blessing from the Master) In this vast universe, across boundless time and space, we are able to live together on this Earth, our home. This is the fruit of our shared karma — we are all connected by affinity. May we cherish this connection and create more wholesome causes and conditions. The power of compassion and loving-kindness, the power of wisdom. May your merit and wisdom increase, and may you be blessed with peace and harmony at all times!
A slow and joyful birthday to you, dear Dharma brother. Light a lamp in the heart — it shines on you and me. Closing my eyes, I pray to the Buddha.
![](media/image8.png){width="3.5433048993875764in" height="0.36909667541557306in"}34
Make a wish — may our parents be healthy and happy. May you be happier than ever before, and may you soon realize Bodhi and attain liberation!
Fullness and Lack
You have been up in the sky for countless years,
shining upon humanity's joys and sorrows, reunions and partings.
I look up and only see
the moon wax and wane, now full, now fading.
This has been so since ancient times — nothing is ever complete.
Longing for the eternal, yet quietly grieving in my heart.
Where is a flower that never changes its form?
What, then, is the true face of the world,
that can set this heart free from confusion?
Without lack, one cannot know fullness; without fullness, one cannot know lack.
Fullness and lack arise from each other — in essence, there is neither fullness nor lack.
The Red Lotus
Nothing can stand in my way
as I walk the path of awakening. A life adrift in samsara —
my heart drifts with the wind.
In those years of ignorance and confusion, I too felt lost and uncertain.
Until I met you once more — only then did the path of awakening unfold.
This world of selfless altruism is so clear, so vast and high,
where an unfading red lotus blooms forever.
In the Name of Bodhi
When you feel
my kindness and warmth,
that is the smile I offer in the name of Bodhi.
![](media/image7.png){width="3.5433048993875764in" height="0.36909667541557306in"}35
When you feel my diligence and courage,
that is my selfless devotion in the name of Bodhi. When you hear my voice of wisdom,
that is the insight I have realized in the name of Bodhi. When you see me with palms joined in reverence,
that is my sincere prayer in the name of Bodhi. Let us don the armour of diligence and walk out of the darkness.
Let us light the lamp and pass it on, illuminating the world.
Let us selflessly serve others, quietly devoting ourselves.
Let us carry forward this culture of wisdom.

The Road Home
The road home — so near yet worlds apart. Trapped in a burning house, eyes brimming with tears.
The road home — twisting and turning a thousand times.
Compassionate Dharma words, a guide for the lost.
The road home — tranquil and transcendent.
Parting the heavy mist, awaiting the wanderer's return. The road home — long or short, it depends.
Along the Mindful Peace Academy, together we return to the home of awakening. Namo Buddhaya, Namo Dharmaya, Namo Sanghaya.
![](media/image8.png){width="3.5433048993875764in" height="0.36909667541557306in"}36
A Dharma joy wells up in my heart. A feeling of gratitude forever stays within. A vow fills my heart with strength. A shared responsibility, you and I bear together. The road home.
Shared Vows, Shared Journey
By the Nairanjana River the ancient path runs deep, beneath the Bodhi tree the Sakyamuni sits. The unexcelled, profound, and subtle Dharma — a thousand years of grace through compassion and wisdom intertwined. The Bodhi home is rich in Dharma bonds, the three-level curriculum offers a fresh model. Through the Eight Steps and Three Types of Meditation we learn the wondrous Dharma, spreading the Mahayana Bodhi mind.
Diligently we study, never growing lax; through work and practice we constantly renew ourselves.
Lamp passes to lamp, together we bathe in the Buddha's grace. Kindling passed from one to the next — shared vows, shared journey. Together we reach the other shore, together we pray: selflessly serving others, benefiting all beings.
We vow to be the Tathagata's messengers, with endless resolve — shared vows, shared journey. Boundless as space, boundless the lamp of the heart — may all be fulfilled as vowed. Shared vows, shared journey.
![](media/image7.png){width="3.5433048993875764in" height="0.36909667541557306in"}37
Mindful Peace Academy: Introductory Study Guide
—Empowering Everyone to Take Initiative in Their Study and Practice
Core Philosophy: Empowering everyone to take initiative in their study and practice · Making study and practice easier · Realizing ourselves in benefiting others
Introduction: How to Use This Guide
What Is This Guide
This is a study roadmap, not a rulebook. It answers one core question: How can you truly benefit from study and practice at Mindful Peace Academy?
Who Is This Guide For
New students: Understand the overall framework and basic methods of study and practice
Continuing students: Use it for self-check and to fill any gaps
Facilitators / class committee members: Use it as a reference manual for guiding students
Core Philosophy: You are the master of your own study and practice. This guide is not here to constrain you, but to help you walk the path of awakening with greater clarity and ease.
Chapter 1: Study and Practice Orientation: Who You Are and Why You're Here
1.1 What Is Mindful Peace Academy
Mindful Peace Academy is a non-profit learning community dedicated to promoting Eastern wisdom, advocating a Chan-inspired way of living, and guiding people toward life awakening.
Three Core Philosophies:

{.table}
-

- + Philosophy

+ What It Means for You

- + Empowering everyone to take initiative in their study and practice

+ You are not passively receiving teachings --- you are actively charting your own path of awakening

- + Empowering everyone to fully contribute

+ Study and practice is not only for self-benefit --- every time you share, every time you step up, you are both bettering yourself and lighting the way for others

- + Making study and practice easier

+ Providing clear methods, practical tools, and a warm environment

![](media/image9.png){width="3.5433048993875764in" height="0.36909667541557306in"}38
