Chapter 3: Methods of Study and Practice: Three Types of Meditation
When the method is right, study and practice become effortless. The core method of Mindful Peace Academy can be summed up as "Eight Steps" and "Three Types of Meditation." Here we explain them in the simplest possible terms.
3.1 The Eight Steps --- Turning "Knowledge" Into "Your Own"
Many people's frustration is: I understand the principles, but I can't apply them. The Eight Steps solve exactly this --- they help you turn the wisdom you learn into your own mindset and habits.

{.table}
-

- + Steps

+ In a Nutshell

+ Have You Done This?

- + Steps 1--2

+ Read thoroughly, understand every sentence

+ □ Basically can do it

- + Steps 3--4

+ Connect the principles to your own life and reflect

+ □ Sometimes can do it

- + Steps 5--6

+ Try applying them in your life, observe the changes

+ □ Ready to start trying

- + Steps 7--8

+ Gradually form new good habits, become a better person

+ □ For later

As a new student, you don't need to master all the steps at once. Just get the first four steps down solidly --- that's already excellent.
> Ask yourself after each lesson:
> - Do I truly understand what this lesson is about?
> - Can I apply this principle in my own life?
![](media/image9.png){width="3.5433048993875764in" height="0.36909667541557306in"}40
3.2 Three Types of Meditation --- Practice Is Like Training a Skill
Meditation is not just "sitting cross-legged." At Mindful Peace Academy, we divide practice into three types, which work together and deepen layer by layer, like training a martial art:
Type of Meditation / What It Solves / Main Method / Your Stage

{.table}
-

- + Eight Steps and Three Types of Meditation

+ Transform mistaken thinking

+ Attend teachings, reflect, connect to reality

+ Throughout the Collective Joy Course

- + Mindfulness Meditation

+ Settle the mind, stop it from wandering

+ Seven Steps of Mindful Tea, observing the breath, mindful living

+ Start beginner level in Collective Joy Course

Altruistic Meditation

Make the heart warmer and kinder

Gratitude, rejoicing, chanting the *Metta Chanting* / Start beginner level in Collective Joy Course

3.3 Mindfulness Meditation --- Settling the Mind in the Present (Introductory Version)
What is mindfulness?
It is simply "knowing what you are doing." Most of the time, we live in unawareness --- thinking about work while eating, thinking about other things while walking, the mind constantly running. Mindfulness is the practice of bringing the mind back and settling it in the present moment.
Beginner-Level Mindfulness Meditation (What You'll Do in the Collective Joy Course)
You don't need to sit cross-legged for a long time. Start with these small things:
- Observing the breath: Spend some time each day quietly feeling your breathing. Know the in-breath, know the out-breath. When thoughts drift away, gently bring them back. No judgment, no rush.
- Seven Steps of Mindful Tea: Slowly make a cup of tea, paying full attention to each movement --- lifting, pouring, smelling, tasting. The whole process is clear and aware.
- Mindfulness in daily life: When walking, know you are walking; when eating, know you are chewing; when brushing your teeth, know you are brushing your teeth.
Key principle: Mindfulness is not "having no thoughts" --- it is "knowing there are thoughts, but not being carried away by them." Thoughts are like clouds in the sky; you are the sky --- clouds come and go, the sky remains still.
Intermediate and Advanced (For Later)
When you enter the Collective Practice Course and Collective Virtue Course, you will further learn how to extend awareness to every corner of life, and even realize the wisdom of "no-thought." No need to worry about that now.
3.4 Altruistic Meditation --- Warming the Heart (Introductory Version)
What is altruism?
It is learning to care for others and treat them kindly. We are used to being self-centered and easily overlook others' feelings. Altruistic meditation helps us open our hearts and move from indifference to warmth.
![](media/image10.png){width="3.5433048993875764in" height="0.36909667541557306in"}41
Beginner-Level Altruistic Meditation (What You'll Do in the Collective Joy Course)
No need to do great things --- start with these small ones:
- Loving-kindness wishes: Recite the *Metta Chanting* each day, silently: "May I be safe and happy, may they be safe and happy, may all beings be safe and happy."
- Gratitude: Each day, think: who has helped me? Say "thank you" in your heart. Parents, friends, fellow students --- gradually you will find there are many people worthy of gratitude.
- Rejoicing: When you see someone do something well, be genuinely happy for them and say "I rejoice in your goodness." No jealousy, no comparison.
- Understanding and acceptance: When someone makes you uncomfortable, try thinking: "They may have their own difficulties too." Let go of opposition.
Key principle: Altruism is not about sacrificing yourself --- it is about your own heart becoming softer and happier in the process of caring for others. Even just pouring a cup of water for a fellow student is practicing altruism.
Intermediate and Advanced (For Later)
Later you will learn about "bodhicitta" --- vowing to help all beings move toward awakening, while not clinging in the process of benefiting others. For now, start with small things.
One last thought: Practice is not mysticism --- it is science. It has methods, stages, and can be verified. You don't need "high natural ability" --- you just need "willingness to repeat." Take it slow, and you'll actually go faster.
