Chapter 5: Approach to Study and Practice: Self-Study and Group Study
5.1 The Relationship Between Self-Study and Group Study

{.table}
-

- + Mode

+ Function

+ Frequency

- + Self-Study

+ Independent study and reflection, building the foundation of right view

+ Daily

- + Small Group Study

+ In-depth discussion, mutual inspiration

+ Once a week

- + Class Group Study

+ Whole-group exchange, guided by the facilitator

+ Once a week

Key insight: Self-study and group study are equally important --- you can't have one without the other. Without self-study, you have nothing to say in group study; without group study, self-study easily goes off track.
5.2 Self-Study Guide
Suggested time:
Collective Joy Course: No less than 40 minutes per day
Collective Practice Course: No less than 1 hour per day
Collective Virtue Course: No less than 1.5 hours per day
Self-study steps:
1. Open Bodhi Navigator → Self-Study → Study Management
2. Study and reflect on the Dharma (watch videos / read e-books)
3. Complete MetaNote (mind maps, problem awareness, lesson problem-solving, etc.)
4. Check in on Bodhi Navigator, read the mental reminder
Self-study guidelines:
Be honest, earnest, committed, and self-motivated
Follow the course schedule, don't skip lessons
Note down questions promptly and raise them during group study
Don't practice blindly on your own; don't chase after scattered interests
5.3 Group Study Guide
Small Group Study Process (once a week, rotating host):
1. Open Bodhi Navigator → Group Study → Study Management
![](media/image9.png){width="3.5433048993875764in" height="0.36909667541557306in"}44
2. Meditation (listen to the *Metta Chanting* or 10 minutes of mindful sitting)
3. Dharma review (host, 5 minutes)
4. Share and discuss based on supporting materials or MetaNote
5. Closing (Four Immeasurables)
6. Check in on Bodhi Navigator, read the mental reminder
Class Group Study Process (once a week):
1. Open Bodhi Navigator → Group Study → Study Management
2. Meditation (listen to the *Metta Chanting* or 10 minutes of mindful sitting)
3. Previous session review (2--3 min) + Current session introduction (7--8 min)
4. Student sharing (5 minutes each, 11 people total)
5. 30-minute equal exchange (core segment)
6. Facilitator summary (5--10 minutes)
7. Closing (assign next session, recite the Five Mindfulness Trainings for Healthy Living, Four Immeasurables, etc.)
8. Check in on Bodhi Navigator, read the mental reminder
5.4 The 30-Minute Equal Exchange --- The Core of Class Group Study
What it is: A free-form discussion around "How has this session's Dharma changed my views / mindset / life?" --- no judging, no going off-topic, no explaining terminology.
How to do it:
The facilitator guides; students speak freely
You may ask: "What did Student A's sharing inspire in you?"
You may respond: "How exactly can this Dharma be applied in daily life?"
Three "Don'ts":
Don't judge: Respect every student's sharing; don't say "you're wrong"
Don't go off-topic: Stay focused on the session's Dharma; don't branch into unrelated subjects
Don't explain terminology: Avoid using technical jargon to overpower others
5.5 Group Study Guidelines
Participation:
![](media/image10.png){width="3.5433048993875764in" height="0.36909667541557306in"}45
Attend on time, don't be late or leave early
If unable to attend: notify in advance, and make up the lesson or submit a practice reflection of 500+ words
Sharing:
Stay focused on the Dharma; share authentic reflections
No idle chatter, no going off-topic
Reflect on your own shortcomings; rejoice in the goodness of others
Atmosphere:
Silence your phone
Speak gently
Maintain purity and dignity
